    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d312">

                <pb facs="0476" n="377" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg377"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.4061 Motion Pictures/101a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-12-24T16:00:00-05:00">December 24, 1936—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>193. Mr. Hays has informed the Department that Smith was told by Reber<anchor
                        n="43" xml:id="d312-f1"/> that the Italian authorities have stated that the
                    film company’s <pb facs="0477" n="378" xml:id="pg378"/> assumptions with regard
                    to Ciano’s note are correct, and also that Livengood will take up with the
                    authorities the question of the manner in which a Hays representative may sit in
                    with regard to the allocation of exportable lire. On the basis of this
                    information the American companies are today ordering their continental managers
                    to resume business in Italy.</p>
                <p>Your 549 last paragraph. Yes. Hays concurs.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Moore</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d312-f1" n="43" xml:id="d312-f1-content">
                <p>Samuel Reber, Second Secretary of Embassy in Italy.</p>
            </note>
